  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nCooler air has arrived in the region as a cold front continues to \npush to the southeast, further into Kansas and Missouri. Expected \nhighs today will be in the 70s. A few showers and thunderstorms \ncontinue to move across central and eastern Nebraska this afternoon, \nbringing a chance for some rain, hail, and gusty winds. MUCAPE \nvalues range from 250-1000 J/kg along and slightly north of the \nKS/NE border. The more unstable environment is to the south of the \narea, but can't entirely rule out a strong to marginally severe \nstorm or two this afternoon, with hail and gusty winds being the \nmain concerns. \n\nHeading into tonight/early Sunday morning, a weak shortwave trough \nmoves across the region. The aforementioned cold front lifts north \ntoward the KS/NE border. Several models are showing the potential \nfor a line of storms to kick off somewhere across southeastern \nNebraska/southwestern Iowa with the greatest concern being around \nthe 11Z-15Z timeframe. Shower and storm chances will gradually end \nwest to east Sunday afternoon. \n\nA ridge moves across the Great Plains Monday bringing a brief return \nof summer-like temperatures. Expect afternoon highs to top out in \nthe 80s for most areas, with a few 90s from Lincoln and Seward south \nto the KS/NE border. An upper low over Saskatchewan and Manitoba \nwith an attendant low move east through the day. Heading into the \nafternoon, there are concerns for a few strong to severe storms as \nthe system moves through and instability increases. \n\nThe cold front arrives Monday night/Tuesday morning, bringing \nanother shot of cooler air to the region. Expect highs each day to \ntop out in the 60s and 70s and lows to fall into the 50s and 60s. \nThere will be daily chances through the end of the forecast period \nfor a few showers and thunderstorms as a series of disturbances move \nthrough the region.",
